WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.770
Rhiannon: We often hear Mental Health
problems discussed regarding adults,

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.620
but over the last five years, surveys of
the mental health of children and young

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.350
people in England found that over 15
percent had at least one mental disorder

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.229
when assessed, and over 5 percent met
the criteria for 2 or more according to

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:19.880
the NHS, and these number are increasing.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.160
Leah: Approximately 50 percent of lifelong
mental health problems, have started by

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.220
the time a young person reaches their
mid-teens, and around 75 percent by

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:37.580
their mid-20s, although most treatment
typically starts a few years later.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.650
Rhiannon: When looking at data on the
mental health of very young children, aged

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:47.059
2 to 4, this is something still rather new
and experimental, but some results suggest

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:51.110
that approximately 5 percent of pre-school
children may have a mental disorder,

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:56.259
with a higher rate in boys than in girls.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.930
Leah: Over half of children and
young people with a probable mental

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.020
disorder report having sleep problems.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.710
In teenage girls two thirds
with a probable mental disorder,

00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:07.910
had seen or heard an argument
among adults in their household.

00:01:08.559 --> 00:01:11.980
Rhiannon: Over half the children at school
age with a probable mental disorder,

00:01:12.279 --> 00:01:14.110
have regular support from their school.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.720
And school age children with a
probable mental disorder are more

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.320
than twice as likely to live in a
household with financial difficulties.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.210
Leah: Children who suffer from
neglect are more likely to experience

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.290
mental health problems including
depression, post-traumatic stress

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.619
disorder and attention deficit
and hyperactivity disorder.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.789
This may impact their relationships
in later life, including

00:01:41.789 --> 00:01:42.780
with their own children.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.520
Rhiannon: Family mental health
problems, a hostile environment and

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:50.320
breakup, are proven to negatively
affect a child’s mental wellbeing.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.480
Mental health problems in children and
young people can include depression,

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.669
anxiety and conduct disorder, and
are often a direct response to

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.100
what is happening in their lives.

00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:03.389
Leah: The emotional wellbeing
of children is just as important

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.519
as their physical health.

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:09.050
Good mental health allows children and
young people to develop the resilience to

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.550
cope with whatever life throws at them and
grow into well-rounded, healthy adults.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.679
Rhiannon: Social media, also now gives
access to information for young people

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:21.810
to find out more about what help is
available, but it can also open doors

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.080
to getting bad advice or misinformation.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:28.620
Leah: 30 years ago, information on mental
health was more restricted to health

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.890
professionals, scholarly articles or
books, whereas today there are websites

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.740
covering all aspects and issues.

00:02:35.259 --> 00:02:38.720
This can have a positive effect in
informing children and young people that

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.779
there is help out there, if they want it.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.183
Rhiannon: But, mismanaged or taken out of
context can also be negative by opening

00:02:45.183 --> 00:02:49.320
them to a world of pitfalls which can have
further impact on their mental health.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.040
Leah: It’s important then, to use
this knowledge and these resources

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.133
to help, while not allowing some of
these same avenues to undermine the

00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:58.310
work we are trying to accomplish.

